"""Original Codex research teaching and four distinct 50-mark practice packages."""
import sys
from pathlib import Path
TD=Path(__file__).resolve().parent;sys.path.insert(0,str(TD.parent))
from manuscript_support import build
E=[
('01-00','Choose an enquiry','A research topic identifies an issue that can be investigated with accessible evidence in a specified setting.','Choose a safe, feasible focus rather than an entire national problem. Learners may collect in groups, but each presents an individual report; the written examination asks about the enquiry, which is not formally assessed coursework.'),
('02-00','A usable topic','A good topic combines interest, relevance, ethical practice and a specific area and time.','Test whether a manageable enquiry can answer it with available resources. Interest alone does not justify sensitive questions or unsafe fieldwork.'),
('02-01','Interest','Interest gives a researcher a reason to sustain attention to an issue.','It should not preselect the answer; record observations that challenge expectations as well as those that agree.'),
('02-02','Relevance','Relevance links an enquiry to a meaningful development issue and the evidence needed.','A water-journey question can inform access, while an unrelated opinion survey may not address that aim.'),
('02-03','Ethics','Ethical enquiry respects consent, privacy, safety and participants’ choice not to take part.','Use non-sensitive topics, explain purpose and avoid identifiable records where unnecessary. Do not pressure participants, enter unsafe sites or ask classmates for health or violence disclosures.'),
('02-04','Specific scope','State the study area, population, variables and collection dates or period.','A bounded question such as school-route travel time in one settlement during one week is more manageable than asking why education is unequal everywhere.'),
('03-00','Aim and objectives','An aim states what the enquiry intends to find out; objectives are specific tasks that help achieve it.','Start with a question, then choose measurable objectives and collection steps. A recommendation is a later response, not an observation already established before collection.'),
('03-01','Research aim','A research aim is a broad statement of what an enquiry intends to find out, linked to its research question or hypothesis.','For a water-access enquiry, the aim might be to investigate return-journey times during the stated week.'),
('03-02','Research objective','A research objective is a specific task or target that helps the enquiry achieve its aim.','For that aim, record return minutes including waiting for selected households and compare the resulting categories.'),
('04-00','Question and hypothesis','A research question asks what the enquiry will investigate; a hypothesis predicts a testable relationship or outcome.','“Do longer routes accompany lower attendance?” is a question; “Longer routes accompany lower attendance” is a hypothesis. Evidence may challenge either expectation.'),
('04-01','Hypothesis','A hypothesis is a tentative, testable statement about a relationship or outcome that evidence can support or challenge.','Specify measurable variables and scope. A claim so vague that no observation could challenge it is unsuitable.'),
('04-02','Research question','A research question is a focused question that the enquiry seeks to answer using evidence.','It need not predict the answer. Its wording should guide variables, participants and method.'),
('05-00','Data origins','Primary data is gathered first-hand for the current enquiry; secondary data was previously collected or published for another purpose.','Classify origin relative to this enquiry, not whether the information is printed, digital, numerical or spoken.'),
('05-01','Primary data','Primary data is data collected first-hand for the present enquiry.','A learner’s own journey observations qualify; accuracy still depends on selection, definitions and recording.'),
('05-02','Secondary data','Secondary data is data already collected or published for another purpose and used in the present enquiry.','A published school register or service table can help if its dates, definitions and population fit; publication does not guarantee suitability.'),
('06-00','Collection methods','Questionnaires, interviews, observation and secondary-source research obtain different kinds of evidence.','Specify who or what, where, when and how it is recorded. A bar chart presents collected data; it does not collect responses.'),
('06-01','Questionnaires','A questionnaire uses a consistent set of questions with recordable answers.','Use neutral wording, one issue per question, units and non-overlapping categories. Pilot it with relevant volunteers, protect privacy and allow refusal.'),
('06-02','Interviews','An interview obtains information by asking participants questions and recording answers consistently.','Explain purpose and consent, use a neutral schedule and ask clarifying questions without suggesting an answer. Interviews can explore reasons but require time and careful recording.'),
('06-03','Observation','Observation records directly visible or measurable features through a planned record.','Define categories, units, site and time before collecting. Count from a safe public place; do not handle waste or infer private motives from visible behaviour.'),
('06-04','Secondary-source research','Secondary-source research selects and records relevant existing evidence.','Note publisher, date, unit, population, method and limitations. A different district or old period may not answer the current local question.'),
('07-00','Choose methods','Evaluate a method by fit to the question, evidence quality, resources and ethics.','Direct observation can measure visible counts but not private reasons; interviews can ask reasons but may face recall or response bias. A researcher difficulty obstructs collection; a community problem is what the enquiry investigates.'),
('08-00','Pilot study','A pilot study is a small preliminary trial of research procedures or instruments used to identify problems and make improvements before the main enquiry.','Try with relevant volunteers, record difficulties, revise the instrument and check the revision. Pilot answers do not establish the whole population’s pattern.'),
('09-00','Population and sample','The research population is the whole defined group; a sample is the selected subset studied.','The sampling method is the selection procedure, not the group itself. Findings need limits where the frame misses groups or responses are incomplete.'),
('09-01','Research population','The research population is the whole group of units or people that the enquiry is intended to investigate.','Define it before sampling: all households on the relevant settlement list differs from everyone in Namibia.'),
('09-02','Research sample','The research sample is the selected subset of the research population from which data are collected.','A list of 12 selected households describes a sample; choosing every fifth household describes the method.'),
('10-00','Sampling choices','Random, stratified and systematic sampling use different explicit selection procedures.','Start with a relevant frame, justify size and selection, record non-response and avoid silently replacing a refusal with a friend. No sample guarantees perfect representativeness.'),
('10-01','Random sampling','Random sampling selects by chance so each member of the defined research population has an equal chance of selection.','Number a complete list and use a fair draw or random numbers without duplicates. Selecting whoever is nearest does not meet that rule.'),
('10-02','Stratified sampling','Stratified sampling divides a population into relevant groups and selects from each using an explicit method.','For proportional selection, allocate sample places by each group’s share, then randomly select within groups. It preserves planned group coverage but cannot remove every frame or response bias.'),
('10-03','Systematic sampling','Systematic sampling selects at a fixed interval from an ordered list after an appropriate starting point.','For 60 households and 12 places, interval is five; a fair start among positions one to five can produce a reproducible selection. A repeating pattern in the list may bias it.'),
('11-00','Organise, present and interpret','Organise records consistently, reconcile totals, then choose a suitable graph or sketch map to answer the enquiry.','A table states observations; interpretation links relevant evidence into a pattern. Percentage equals category count divided by the relevant total, multiplied by 100; identify missing data and avoid asserting causation from association alone.'),
('11-01','Findings','Findings report and interpret collected evidence, including comparisons and exceptions relevant to the aim.','A high value differs from a large increase. Use paired observations and units rather than a list with no relationship.'),
('11-02','Graphs','Bar charts compare categories; pie charts or divided rectangles show parts of a stated whole when categories are compatible.','For bars state axes, units, category labels and an even scale. Pie angle is category share multiplied by 360°; a divided rectangle allocates proportional lengths. Totals must reconcile. Graphs here use data and construction specifications, not an unseen verified rendering.'),
('11-03','Sketch maps','A sketch map locates relevant features and patterns using clear symbols and a key, title and orientation.','Use observed or supplied relative positions; do not imply accurate scale unless measured. Distinguish a spatial pattern from a graph of category totals.'),
('12-00','Conclusions and recommendations','A conclusion answers the question from findings within their limits; a recommendation proposes a feasible response tied to that answer and identifies an implementer.','Do not introduce uncollected evidence. For community action distinguish aims, organisation, funding and implementation obstacles: assigning volunteers does not obtain money, and an obstacle needs a matched response and responsible actor.'),
('12-01','Conclusion','A conclusion is an evidence-based answer to the research question or judgement about the hypothesis, within the enquiry’s limits.','A sample pattern can support a bounded answer, not a claim about all places. Findings are the supporting observations, not interchangeable with the conclusion.'),
('12-02','Recommendation','A recommendation is a proposed feasible action linked to the findings and conclusions, identifying who could implement it.','Name the practical change, implementer and relevant resource condition. A wish such as “improve development” is too vague to follow.')]
L={s:(m,e) for s,h,m,e in E};Q={}
def q(n,p,a,g):Q[n]=(p,a,g)
q(1,'Identify the research topic in “Return water-collection time for households in fictional Omi settlement during one stated week.”','Household water-collection time in the specified settlement and week.',['Feature: issue with bounded place and time.'])
q(2,'Suggest a feasible topic for learners who can safely observe an accessible public bus stop for two afternoons but cannot interview travellers.','Compare recorded bus arrival intervals at that stop on the two stated afternoons, with a safe approved observation point. It fits accessible evidence and avoids unsupported questions about private reasons.',['Context: limited time and observation-only access.','Reason: match topic to feasible safe evidence.'])
q(3,'Describe two features of a good research topic.','A specific manageable focus and ethical collection with relevant evidence.',['Feature: two meaningful topic criteria.'])
q(4,'Explain why a topic needs more than personal interest.','It also needs safe feasible evidence and a bounded question; interest cannot make an intrusive or unanswerable enquiry suitable.',['Reason: interest linked to feasibility and ethics.'])
for n,s in [(5,'02-01'),(6,'02-02'),(7,'02-03'),(8,'02-04'),(18,'05-00')]:q(n,'Describe two considerations concerning '+dict((x[0],x[1]) for x in E)[s].lower()+'.',' '.join(L[s]),['Feature: '+L[s][0],'Feature: '+L[s][1]])
q(9,'Identify whether “record selected households’ journey minutes” is an aim or a specific objective.','A specific research objective.',['Feature: operational task serving an aim.'])
q(10,'Describe how to turn a fictional water-access question into an aim and measurable objectives.','Specify return journeys in the settlement during the week; set an aim to investigate time differences, then objectives to record minutes including waiting, group results consistently and compare categories.',['Sequence: question to aim to measurable collection/comparison tasks.','Application: water-journey variables, units and period.'])
for n,s in [(11,'03-01'),(12,'03-02'),(15,'04-01'),(16,'04-02'),(19,'05-01'),(20,'05-02'),(33,'08-00'),(38,'09-01'),(39,'09-02')]:q(n,'Define '+dict((x[0],x[1]) for x in E)[s].lower()+'.',L[s][0],['Meaning: '+L[s][0]])
q(13,'Differentiate a research question and a hypothesis.','A question asks what is to be investigated; a hypothesis states a testable expectation that evidence can support or challenge.',['Comparison: interrogative enquiry versus testable prediction.'])
q(14,'Suggest a testable hypothesis where a fictional school can record route lengths and attendance using approved non-identifying records.','Longer school routes are associated with lower attendance in the specified group and period. The two recorded variables can support or challenge it; they do not by themselves prove cause.',['Context: usable route and attendance evidence.','Reason: explicit testable relationship.'])
q(61,'Explain why “Does distance relate to attendance?” is not itself a hypothesis.','It asks a question without predicting the relationship. A hypothesis would state a testable expected association; test question form versus an evidence-challengeable prediction.',['Error: question treated as prediction.','Distinction: focused question versus hypothesis.'])
q(17,'Differentiate primary and secondary data for a new local enquiry.','Primary data is collected first-hand for this enquiry; secondary data already exists from another collection or purpose. Format alone does not decide it.',['Comparison: origin relative to the present enquiry.'])
q(21,'Name one collection method for recording directly visible litter categories.','Structured observation.',['Feature: a collection method, not a display.'])
q(22,'Name and describe a way to collect fictional household water-journey times when selected adults freely consent.','Use a questionnaire: explain purpose and privacy, ask return minutes including waiting for a stated recent journey, record the numerical response with date and non-identifying code, and allow refusal.',['Sequence: consent, consistent question and recording.','Application: water-journey unit and time reference.'])
proc={23:('a questionnaire','selected households’ return water journeys','Ask a neutral single-issue question in minutes including waiting, use an anonymous code and date, record replies consistently and allow refusal.'),25:('an interview','why selected consenting adult volunteers use different water points','Use a neutral schedule at an agreed safe time, ask which point and reasons without suggesting an answer, record consented responses consistently and omit names.'),27:('structured observation','litter visible from two safe public paths','Define litter categories, observation length and time, count visible items on each route using the same record and do not handle waste or enter unsafe areas.'),29:('secondary-source research','whether an existing school table fits a local enquiry','Locate the original publisher, record dates, age groups, units and collection definitions, then compare its scope with the present question and note mismatches.'),40:('systematic sampling','12 households from a complete ordered list of 60','Use interval five; draw a start fairly from one to five, then take each fifth entry. With start three choose 3,8,13,18,23,28,33,38,43,48,53,58 and record refusals.'),42:('random sampling','five entries from a complete list of 20','Number entries one to 20, use a fair draw or random-number procedure for five distinct numbers, map them to entries and record selection and non-response.'),44:('stratified sampling','12 households from groups of 40 and 20','Allocate eight and four places proportionally, then fairly select distinct numbered households within each group. Record frame coverage and refusals rather than substitute convenient respondents.'),46:('systematic sampling','ten records from a complete ordered list of 50','Set interval five, choose a fair start among one to five and take every fifth record until ten are selected; inspect the list for periodic patterns.')}
for n,(method,case,a) in proc.items():q(n,'Name and describe '+method+' for this fictional task: '+case+'.',method.capitalize()+': '+a,['Sequence: explicit reproducible steps.','Application: '+a])
for n,s in [(24,'06-01'),(26,'06-02'),(28,'06-03'),(30,'06-04'),(31,'07-00'),(41,'10-00'),(43,'10-01'),(45,'10-02'),(47,'10-03')]:q(n,'Explain one reason or limitation relevant to '+dict((x[0],x[1]) for x in E)[s].lower()+'.',' '.join(L[s]),['Reason: '+L[s][1]])
q(60,'Explain why drawing a bar chart does not collect people’s reasons for choosing a water point.','A chart presents already obtained information. A suitable interview or questionnaire obtains reasons; test obtaining observations versus displaying results.',['Error: presentation substitutes for collection.','Distinction: collecting versus displaying.'])
q(32,'Explain which method fits an invented enquiry about visible litter counts on safe public paths: direct structured observation or interviews asking residents to remember counts. Time and safe access are available.','Observation more directly measures visible counts with consistent categories. Interviews may explore reasons but recall is weaker evidence for exact site counts; choose by the actual variable and access conditions.',['Judgement: select against accurate visible counts.','Reason: weigh direct measurement and recalled counts.'])
q(64,'Explain why a researcher’s broken recording device and a community’s long water journey need different responses.','The device obstructs collection and may need a replacement record; the journey is the development issue under study. Test what prevents the enquiry versus what the enquiry investigates.',['Error: community problem substituted for research difficulty.','Distinction: collection obstacle versus development issue, with matched responses.'])
q(34,'Describe a pilot for an invented water questionnaire whose draft asks whether journeys are “long”.','Try it with relevant volunteers, note requests to define long, replace it with return minutes including waiting, and retry to check understanding before main collection.',['Sequence: trial, recorded difficulty, revision and recheck.','Application: ambiguous water-journey wording repaired.'])
q(35,'Suggest a repair when four of six fictional pilot volunteers interpret “long journey” differently.','Ask return minutes including waiting for a stated journey and retry it with relevant volunteers. A common measure reduces the ambiguity that the pilot revealed.',['Context: divergent interpretations.','Reason: comparable recording and recheck.'])
q(36,'Differentiate research population and sample.','The population is the whole defined group; the sample is the selected subset from which evidence is gathered.',['Comparison: whole group versus studied subset.'])
q(37,'Suggest a suitable population definition for an invented enquiry into households using one settlement water point during a stated week.','Define all households using that point in the settlement and week, with an appropriate way to identify them before selection. National residents are too broad for that question.',['Context: one point, settlement and period.','Reason: frame fits the intended inference.'])
q(59,'Explain why “the 12 selected households” names a sample rather than a sampling method.','It identifies the studied group. A procedure such as selecting every fifth household names the method; test selected units versus the selection steps.',['Error: group substituted for procedure.','Distinction: sample versus sampling method.'])
q(48,'Describe the relationship in this original fictional table: near-route households, 8 of 10 journeys below 20 minutes; distant-route households, 3 of 10 below 20.','Short journeys are more common in the near group, 80% versus 30%, a 50-percentage-point difference. The association does not isolate distance from waiting or route conditions.',['Evidence: compare both group denominators.','Relationship: route category and short-journey prevalence.'])
q(49,'Calculate the share of 24 fictional journeys taking at least 30 minutes if six meet that condition.','6 ÷ 24 = 0.25; multiplying by 100 gives 25%.',['Working: category divided by total then converted.','Unit: percent of recorded journeys.'])
q(50,'Describe how to organise and draw a bar graph for fictional journey categories: below 10 minutes four, 10–29 five, 30 or more three.','Check the total is 12; place non-overlapping categories horizontally and households vertically with scale 0–5 at unit intervals. Draw separated equal-width bars of heights 4,5,3 with title, units and labels; read the largest category and compare relevant counts.',['Sequence: reconcile, axes/scale, bars and interpretation.','Application: exact supplied values and non-overlapping categories.'])
q(62,'Explain why “eight of ten near-route journeys are short” is a finding rather than a complete conclusion about all water access.','It reports observed evidence for one group. A conclusion must answer the specified question using relevant findings and limits; the observation alone cannot establish every household’s experience.',['Error: single finding treated as unrestricted answer.','Distinction: evidence versus bounded conclusion.'])
q(51,'Describe this fictional enquiry result: 12 of 20 respondents report interrupted supply and 8 report reliable supply; 9 of those 12 also report a longer alternative journey.','Interrupted supply is reported by a majority, 60%, and longer alternatives are common within that group, nine of 12. The observations link reliability and journey burden in this sample without showing every interruption has the same effect.',['Evidence: use both total and conditional counts.','Relationship: supply category and alternative journey burden.'])
q(52,'Describe how to construct a pie graph from original fictional counts: walk 6, bus 3, other 3 out of 12.','Convert shares to angles: walk 180°, bus 90°, other 90°, totalling 360°. Draw one circle, measure adjacent sectors and add a title, labels/key and the total and period; do not use unequal totals as directly comparable pies.',['Sequence: shares, angles, sectors and labels.','Application: correct 180/90/90 model specification.'])
q(53,'Describe a sketch map for a fictional 200 m east-west road with a school at the west end, water point at its midpoint and litter site just north of the east end.','Orient north upwards; draw the road west to east, place school left, water point midway and litter site above the right end. Add distinct symbols, a key and title; state that positions are schematic unless measured to scale.',['Sequence: orientation, relative locations, key/title.','Application: map positions, not a category chart.'])
q(54,'Describe what follows from this fictional enquiry: 15 of 20 households report queues over 20 minutes at the same point; five report shorter queues. The aim is to identify whether long queues are common in this sample.','Long queues are common among the sampled households, affecting 75%, but five provide counterexamples. The conclusion fits the sample and period, not a claim that all households always face long queues.',['Evidence: majority and exception.','Relationship: answer the aim within scope.'])
q(55,'Explain which recommendation better fits fictional evidence of repeated water-point breakdowns with a trained maintenance provider available: a provider-led repair schedule or an unrelated poster campaign alone.','The repair schedule directly addresses the observed reliability fault and has an implementer. Posters may share information but do not repair equipment; feasibility and continued fault records should be checked.',['Judgement: choose against the evidenced reliability aim.','Reason: compare matched physical response and information alone.'])
q(56,'Suggest a recommendation where fictional results show unsafe litter near a public path and the responsible council has a collection team but lacks a site report.','Submit the non-identifying location record and request a council-led safe collection and maintenance response. Do not ask learners to handle waste; the recommendation links evidence to the competent implementer.',['Context: location evidence and available collection team.','Reason: matched feasible action.'])
q(63,'Explain why appointing volunteers does not itself fund a fictional N$600 storage project.','It organises people and tasks. Funding obtains money or in-kind resources to meet costs; test responsibility allocation separately from the resource contribution.',['Error: organisation substituted for funding.','Distinction: tasks versus obtaining resources.'])
q(57,'Describe a bounded conclusion from a fictional route survey: longer routes have 50% attendance compared with 80% for shorter routes, but one longer-route school has 90%.','Lower attendance accompanies longer routes overall in the supplied groups, with a notable longer-route exception. This supports an association, not proof of distance causing attendance or a universal rule.',['Evidence: main contrast and exception.','Relationship: conclusion answers the route-attendance question within causal limits.'])
q(58,'Suggest a response where an invented researcher cannot safely reach one sampled route but equivalent information may be obtained from an approved existing route record.','Consult the supervising teacher about using the suitable record, check its date and definitions, and disclose the changed method and coverage limit. Do not enter the unsafe route or silently invent observations.',['Context: researcher safety/access difficulty and possible secondary evidence.','Reason: ethical feasible repair with stated limitations.'])

# Package sources, questions and models are original. Section B explicitly
# transfers to the learner's own enquiry; fictional models never impersonate it.
TRANSFER=' Section B: use your own completed enquiry and real records. If it is not yet completed, state that and use these as preparation prompts; do not invent fieldwork. The model below is fictional and illustrates answer structure only.'
S1='Fictional Omi water enquiry: population is 60 listed households using the shared point, sample 12. List order is fixed with no known five-entry pattern; random start is 3. Pilot: four of six volunteers ask what “long” means. Main records: return journey including waiting, below 10 minutes 4 households, 10–29 minutes 5, 30+ minutes 3. All 12 consented; no names recorded. Proposed community storage activity costs N$600; N$200 is available, six volunteers and a qualified provider can check siting and use. A lid supplier may be late.'
S2='Fictional rural-production enquiry: 60 listed grower households, village A 40 and B 20; sample 12, proportionally allocated. Consenting growers answer an interviewer’s neutral price questions and researchers observe sealed labelled output records with permission. A damaged route blocks one visit; approved appointments at a safe meeting point are possible. Earlier output 100 bags, later 130; resource-use shares for the later operation: water 40%, land preparation 35%, storage 25%. Earlier 20 bags spoiled, later 10, with unchanged definitions. These observations do not isolate all causes. Pilot exposed inconsistent bag sizes; the revision specifies the same labelled standard bag. Field record needs date, village code, standard-bag output, price per standard bag and visible storage condition, with no names.'
S3='Fictional school-access enquiry: full list of 20 schools in one district, sample five selected by fair distinct-number draw. The aim is to examine route length and attendance in one week. Mode counts for 12 recorded journeys: walk 6, bus 3, other 3. Group attendance: shorter routes 80%, longer routes 50%; School R on a longer route records 90%, an exception. A pilot repaired an unclear one-way/return instruction. Community book project: 30 donated usable books, six volunteers, council permission available, N$100 record/material cost with N$40 already available. Wet storage threatens books; a school offers a dry room. The project must have fair lending access and records.'
S4='Fictional community-waste enquiry: two safe public paths, east and west, surveyed at the same time on two days. Do not touch waste. A 200 m east-west road has school at west end, water point at midpoint and litter site north of east end; north is upward in the sketch specification. Collection periods show 30 then 18 visible litter items. One initially selected site is unsafe and must not be entered. Hypothesis: “overflow occurs only where bins are absent”; a supplied observation records overflow beside an existing full bin. Service stimulus: east path has 12 scattered items and no bin, west has 6 beside a full bin; a collection team and N$300 are available. Proposal A funds one new bin with no emptying plan; B funds a collection schedule and a suitable targeted bin if costs allow. Pilot clarified the category “other” with examples. Local observation is accessible but covers few places and times.'

# Each (prompt, answer) is aligned in order with its approved performance part.
PARTS={
'P01':[
('Identify the enquiry population.','All 60 listed households using the shared point.'),
('Design five questionnaire elements to measure the stated journey enquiry.','Non-identifying household code; collection date; water point used; return minutes including waiting for the stated journey; whether that journey included an interruption/alternative, with clear yes/no and optional explanation. Use neutral wording and consent before questions.'),
('Describe systematic selection for the supplied frame.','Interval five, fair start among 1–5; with start 3 select 3,8,13,18,23,28,33,38,43,48,53,58. Record refusals without convenient substitutions.'),
('Explain consent and privacy for this survey.','Explain the aim and voluntary participation, allow refusal, omit names and keep coded records securely; report aggregate findings.'),
('Suggest the pilot wording repair and its benefit.','Replace long with return minutes including waiting for a stated journey, then retry it; this gives a common measure.'),
('Calculate the share taking 30 minutes or more, showing working.','3 ÷ 12 = 0.25; multiplying by 100 gives 25%.'),
('Draw a suitable bar chart of the journey categories.','Title and period stated; x-axis categories below 10,10–29,30+ minutes; y-axis households from 0–5 in ones. Equal-width separated bars have heights 4,5,3; labels and units shown.'),
('Describe the journey pattern using the chart data.','The 10–29 group is largest at five, two more than the 30+ group. Eight of 12 take at least ten minutes, so substantial journey time is common in this sample; not every household has the same journey.'),
('State two feasible storage-activity aims.','Provide appropriately checked local storage access and reduce interruption-related collection difficulty; confirm suitability with the provider.'),
('Describe how to organise the activity.','Agree permission and provider check, allocate volunteers to coordination, records and logistics, set dates and maintenance responsibility, and communicate access arrangements.'),
('Suggest feasible funding for the stated cost.','Use the available N$200 and seek N$400 in agreed donations or suitable in-kind contributions covering actual costs; keep transparent records.'),
('Explain a response to the lid-supply obstacle and name the implementer.','The coordinator should confirm delivery or arrange a provider-approved suitable alternative before use; do not operate incomplete storage merely to meet a date.'),
('State your actual topic, area and linked aim.','Fictional model only: Omi return water-journey time during the defined week, aiming to investigate access burden. Actual answer must use the learner’s own area and aim.'),
('Explain your actual pilot finding and resulting change.','Fictional model: long was ambiguous, so the instrument specified return minutes including waiting and was retried. Use the actual recorded revision.'),
('Describe your actual collection procedure.','Fictional model: define the 60-household frame, select every fifth after a fair start, request consent and record consistent journey minutes in coded records during the week; note coverage limits.'),
('Describe your findings with evidence and implications.','Fictional model: five in the middle category, four shortest and three longest; eight of 12 take at least ten minutes. The sample suggests a time burden, but cannot establish other settlements or all seasons.'),
('Suggest a recommendation and implementer tied to your actual findings.','Fictional model: provider and local committee assess practical access and reliable maintained provision; investigate the recorded burden before committing a particular storage solution.')],
'P02':[
('Name the collection method used for the growers’ price answers.','Interview.'),
('Name and describe interview and observation for the two data needs.','Interview consenting selected growers about price per standard bag with a neutral schedule; observe permitted labelled output records using a consistent dated sheet. Do not infer prices from appearance.'),
('Describe a proportional stratified sample.','Allocate eight places to A and four to B, then randomly choose distinct household numbers within each complete group list; record refusals.'),
('Explain the sampling fit and a limit.','Both villages enter in population proportions, but incomplete lists or non-response can still distort the sample; it is not a guarantee of population truth.'),
('Suggest a safe response to the damaged-route obstacle.','Arrange approved safe meeting appointments for selected consenting growers, disclose the change and record whether it reduces coverage; do not enter the unsafe route.'),
('Design an observation/record sheet for the stated variables.','Columns: date, village and anonymous unit code, standard-bag output count, price per standard bag from the interview, visible storage category and missing-data note. Mark source of price separately from observed records.'),
('Calculate the change in production, showing working.','130 - 100 = 30 additional standard bags.'),
('Describe the two-period production and loss pattern.','Output rises by 30 while spoiled bags fall by ten. Usable bags rise from 80 to 120, so production and reduced loss both improve the supplied result.'),
('Draw a divided rectangle of the resource shares.','Use a 20 cm rectangle of constant width: water 8 cm, land preparation 7 cm, storage 5 cm. Boundaries at 8 and 15 cm; label 40%,35%,25%, title, period and key; total 20 cm/100%.'),
('Explain a plausible resource/output relationship and its limit.','Storage is a recorded resource use and lower spoilage accompanies more usable output. The pair is consistent with better handling, but other conditions may differ and the shares alone do not prove causation.'),
('Suggest an improvement to the stated recording problem.','Use the standard-bag definition throughout, distinguish observed output from reported price and record missing values; the coordinator should check sheets before comparison.'),
('Explain two actual reasons for your research issue selection.','Fictional model: relevant saleable-output losses and accessible non-sensitive records made a bounded production question feasible. Actual reasons must reflect the learner’s choices.'),
('Describe your actual sampling and its fit.','Fictional model: proportionate A/B allocation followed by random within-group selection, protecting village coverage but retaining frame/non-response limits.'),
('Describe findings, evidence, conclusion and limits.','Fictional model: 100 to 130 output and 20 to 10 losses yield 80 to 120 saleable. Useful output improved in the records, but these two periods do not isolate the cause or establish every farm’s experience.'),
('Suggest a matched response to a difficulty you actually encountered.','Fictional model: arrange a safe approved meeting after the route became inaccessible, recording the method and coverage change; this addresses collection rather than declaring the community road problem solved.')],
'P03':[
('State an aim and measurable objective for the school enquiry.','Aim: investigate route length and attendance during the week. Objective: record comparable route categories and attendance percentages for selected schools.'),
('Describe random selection from the supplied school list.','Number the 20 schools, use a fair draw or random numbers for five distinct entries, record selected codes and non-response; do not choose the nearest five.'),
('Explain a suitable collection method for attendance and why.','Use approved non-identifying school attendance records for the same week because they provide the relevant counts; check definitions and permission, and avoid individual names.'),
('Design five interview elements for comparable attendance-access information.','Consenting school staff: school code; stated week; eligible learner total; attendance count or rate with its definition; route-length category and how measured. Record sources and missing values, without identifiable learner details.'),
('Calculate the walking share’s pie angle, showing working.','6 ÷ 12 = 0.5; 0.5 × 360 = 180 degrees.'),
('Draw the travel-mode pie chart.','One circle with walking 180°, bus 90°, other 90°, totalling 360°. Title with period and 12 journeys, clear labels/key and accurately measured adjacent sectors.'),
('Describe the route-attendance pattern with evidence.','Longer-route group attendance is 50% versus 80% for shorter routes, a 30-point gap. School R’s 90% shows an important exception; mode counts alone do not prove distance caused attendance.'),
('Identify the exception to a simple longer-route/lower-attendance rule.','School R has a longer route and 90% attendance.'),
('State two achievable book-project aims.','Improve fair access to the 30 usable donated books and maintain an accountable lending arrangement.'),
('Describe the project organisation.','Obtain permission, allocate volunteers to checking, dry storage, lending records and communication, agree fair lending rules and designate ongoing responsibility.'),
('Suggest funding or in-kind support for the stated costs.','Use N$40 and seek N$60 or appropriate record materials in kind; donated books and the offered room cover different needs, so do not count them as cash automatically.'),
('Explain a matched response to wet storage.','The school and coordinator should arrange the offered dry room before accepting books, with safe storage responsibility; more book donations alone leave damage unresolved.'),
('State your actual question/hypothesis and one objective.','Fictional model: is route length associated with attendance? Record the same-week variables for selected schools. Use the learner’s actual question and objective in the real answer.'),
('Explain the fit of your actual presentation method.','Fictional model: a pie shows compatible travel categories totalling 12; a separate comparison table shows attendance and the exception. Actual justification must match the learner’s variables.'),
('Describe and compare actual findings using evidence.','Fictional model: 80% versus 50% attendance, with School R at 90%; six walk and three each use bus/other. Distinguish the sources rather than pretending modes prove distance effects.'),
('Outline conclusions answering your question within limits.','Fictional model: supplied grouped records support an association with an exception, not a universal or causal rule. The sample and week bound the conclusion.'),
('Suggest a feasible recommendation and implementer.','Fictional model: school and relevant service planners investigate documented route barriers, including the exception, before selecting a transport change; book access uses agreed school/community roles.')],
'P04':[
('Define a research sample.','The selected subset of the research population from which data is collected.'),
('Describe safe recording of visible waste categories on the stated paths.','Define item categories and equal observation lengths/times, record anonymous site codes and visible counts from safe public positions, and mark inaccessible sections as missing rather than zero.'),
('Name and describe two methods for counts and service information.','Structured observation records visible items consistently; a consenting provider interview records collection schedules and capacity with a neutral schedule. Different needs justify different sources.'),
('Explain a selection decision and remaining bias.','Selecting both east and west paths covers contrasting service situations, but two accessible paths can omit other neighbourhoods and observation times.'),
('Suggest a response to the unsafe selected site.','Stop that visit, consult the supervising teacher about safe alternatives or suitable existing evidence, and record changed coverage. Do not enter or silently replace it with a convenient site.'),
('Draw a sketch map using the supplied locations.','North up; road west-east; school at west end, water point midway, litter site north of east end. Include distinct symbols/key and title, and state schematic positions rather than an unmeasured accurate scale.'),
('Describe the mapped spatial pattern.','The litter site is near the east end and north of the road, away from the school at the west end; the water point is between them. The source supports relative locations, not unmeasured pollution effects.'),
('Calculate the absolute count change, showing working.','18 - 30 = -12 items, a reduction of 12 visible items.'),
('Identify evidence challenging the no-bin-only explanation.','Overflow is observed beside an existing full bin.'),
('Describe the second service stimulus.','East has 12 scattered items without a bin; west has six beside a full bin. Absence and unemptied capacity are different possible service issues; counts alone do not isolate causes.'),
('Explain which proposal better fits reliable waste management.','B addresses emptying as well as a targeted missing bin within costs. A adds a bin but leaves collection unresolved, which matters because overflow already occurs beside a full one.'),
('Suggest a feasible action, obstacle response and implementer.','The responsible collection team should schedule emptying and assess a suitable targeted bin within N$300, using permission and cost checks. If costs exceed funds, phase the action rather than promise unfunded equipment; students report locations without handling waste.'),
('Describe advantages and disadvantages of your local enquiry.','Fictional model: local access makes repeat observation manageable and supports contextual knowledge, but a few safe paths/times may not represent the whole community and familiarity can bias selection.'),
('Explain an actual pilot adjustment.','Fictional model: clarify the other-waste category with examples after inconsistent trial coding, then retry; actual answers need the real recorded difficulty and change.'),
('Describe findings, implications and a supported conclusion.','Fictional model: visible count falls from 30 to 18; east/west show different provision issues, and a full-bin overflow challenges absence as the sole cause. Collection reliability merits investigation, but sites, times and observational evidence limit causal inference.'),
('Suggest a response to your actual researcher obstacle.','Fictional model: record inaccessible coverage and agree safe suitable evidence with the supervisor. This repairs enquiry access rather than claiming the community’s waste service has been solved.')]
}

def performance_manuscripts():
    from authoring_support import read
    specs=[s for s in read(TD.parents[1]/'curriculum/performance-slot-specs.json')['slots'] if s['topic_id']=='10.1']
    sources={'P01':S1,'P02':S2,'P03':S3,'P04':S4};result={}
    for spec in specs:
        key=spec['slot'].rsplit('-',1)[-1];rows=PARTS[key];assert len(rows)==len(spec['subparts'])
        prompts=[];answers=[];guidance=[];section_counts={}
        for part,(prompt,answer) in zip(spec['subparts'],rows):
            sec=part['section'];section_counts[sec]=section_counts.get(sec,0)+1;label=f'{sec}({section_counts[sec]})'
            prompts.append(f'{label} {prompt} [{part["mark_tariff"]}]')
            answers.append(label+' '+answer)
            guidance.append(f'{label}, up to {part["mark_tariff"]} marks: '+answer+' Credit the essential method, evidence and matched response as applicable; equivalent accurate approaches are valid.')
        result[key]=(['06-00','10-00','11-00','12-00'],sources[key]+TRANSFER+'\n'+'\n'.join(prompts),'\n'.join(answers),
          ['Original package: Section A 35 marks (A1+A2), Section B 15, total 50. The model Section B is fictional; actual answers must describe the learner’s real enquiry. Guidance is indicative, not an official scheme.',
           {'P01':'Method, evidence and response: apply the instrument and selection to the water aim, interpret the recorded journeys and match project actions to cost and implementation barriers.',
            'P02':'Evidence and meaning: distinguish output and losses, use the village sampling frame and explain what the two periods support without claiming an isolated cause.',
            'P03':'Evidence, reason and response: justify the method and pie representation, retain the school exception and match the book-project plan to the actual resources and storage obstacle.',
            'P04':'Findings, evidence and conclusion: report count and site contrasts, retain the full-bin counterexample and draw a bounded service conclusion; match the practical response to safe access and collection.'}[key]]+guidance)
    return result

P=performance_manuscripts()
examples=[(n,'Worked enquiry connection',Q[k][0]+' '+Q[k][1],[s for s in L if s.startswith(n+'-')]) for n,k in [('01',2),('02',4),('03',10),('04',14),('05',17),('06',22),('07',32),('08',34),('09',37),('10',44),('11',50),('12',55)]]
examples += [('10','Sampling calculations and frames','For the Omi source in Package 1, 60 divided by 12 gives interval five; a fair starting position three gives 3,8,13,18,23,28,33,38,43,48,53,58. The rural source in Package 2 has group proportions 40/60 and 20/60: a 12-unit proportional allocation gives eight and four, followed by random selection within each. In Package 3, a fair draw of five distinct school numbers is random selection; the actual sample is the schools selected, not the drawing procedure. Record non-response and frame limitations.',['10-00','10-01','10-02','10-03']),('11','Check representation by construction','For 12 journeys with walk six, bus three and other three, use a pie with 180°,90°,90° sectors. For resource shares 40%,35%,25%, a 20 cm divided rectangle has lengths 8,7,5 cm. A sketch map uses the supplied road, school, midpoint water point and northeast litter site with orientation and a key. These are explicit model specifications; they do not validate an unseen plotted image. A research-process flow chart can use boxes for question, plan/ethics, pilot, collection, presentation, conclusion and response, with a decision after the pilot returning to revision when needed. Its arrows show sequence and a feedback loop, not measured quantities.',['11-00','11-02','11-03']),('12','Organisation, funding and implementation','A fictional storage activity costing N$600 with N$200 available needs N$400 additional resources. Agreeing volunteers and dates organises it; recording contributions funds it. If a required lid is late, the coordinator should arrange a provider-approved suitable alternative or delay use. For a book project costing N$100 with N$40 available, seek N$60 or suitable in-kind materials and use the offered dry room to prevent the stated storage loss. These are project plans, not evidence that either was implemented. A recommendation identifies an implementer and follows findings; a conclusion does not introduce a new result.',['12-00','12-01','12-02'])]
links={n:'Connect the enquiry to its actual development topic and to Population (7.1) for denominators. Keep an individual record of the real procedure, changes, findings and limits. Fictional models teach the method and must not be presented as the learner’s completed fieldwork.' for n in [f'{i:02d}' for i in range(1,13)]}
if __name__=='__main__':build(TD,E,Q,P,links,examples)
